<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP dependency-check suppressions for the SCA gate.

  There are two kinds of entry here and they are NOT the same decision. Keep them in their own sections:

    1. FALSE POSITIVES — the scanner matched the wrong product. Nothing is being risk-accepted; the finding was
       never about our dependency in the first place. Suppress the mis-matched <cpe>, not the CVEs it drags in:
       one wrong CPE produces an open-ended list of CVEs, and enumerating today's list silently lets tomorrow's
       through. No `until` — a CPE mismatch does not expire, and an expiry here would re-break the build on a
       schedule for a finding that was never real.

    2. RISK ACCEPTANCES — the finding is real and we ship anyway. These carry `until` and come back for review.

  A risk acceptance is a decision to ship a known vulnerability, so each one carries its justification in the
  file rather than in a commit message nobody re-reads:

    <suppress until="2027-01-01Z">
      <notes><![CDATA[
        CVE-2025-XXXXX — <what the CVE is>.
        Not reachable: <the concrete reason this code path is never executed by BookLoom>.
        Reviewed by <name> on <date>. Revisit when <upstream fix / removal of the dependency>.
      ]]></notes>
      <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/com\.example/thing@.*$</packageUrl>
      <cve>CVE-2025-XXXXX</cve>
    </suppress>

  Rules for adding one, per `docs/specification/05_Dependencies/02_DEPENDENCY_POLICY.md#owasp-dependency-check`
  ("upgraded, replaced, or explicitly risk-accepted with justification before merge"):

    * Upgrade or replace FIRST. A suppression is the third option, not the cheap one.
    * Suppress the specific CVE on the specific coordinate — never a bare `<packageUrl>` with no `<cve>`, which
      blanket-suppresses every future finding on that dependency including ones nobody has reviewed.
    * Always set `until`. An expiring suppression comes back for review; a permanent one becomes furniture.
    * Never raise `failBuildOnCVSS` in `build.gradle.kts` to make a finding go away. That silences the finding
      and every other one at that severity, project-wide, invisibly.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!-- ==========================================================================================
       1. FALSE POSITIVES
       ========================================================================================== -->

  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      FALSE POSITIVE — not a risk acceptance.

      `javafx-graphics` bundles the `com.sun.javafx.*` / `com.sun.glass.*` implementation packages. The
      dependency-check evidence collector reads those package names as VENDOR evidence and emits `sun` and
      `oracle` alongside the correct `javafx`, producing three LOW-confidence CPE matches:

          cpe:2.3:a:oracle:javafx:26.0.2    <- CORRECT, deliberately NOT suppressed
          cpe:2.3:a:oracle:openjdk:26.0.2   <- wrong product, suppressed below
          cpe:2.3:a:sun:openjdk:26.0.2      <- wrong product, suppressed below

      The two `openjdk` CPEs match JavaFX 26.0.2 against the JRE itself, pulling in seven 2009-era Sun Java SE
      vulnerabilities (JMX, Swing, Win32 PL&F, X11 — CVE-2009-2475/2476/2689/3879/3881/3882/3883, all >= 7.0)
      that describe Java SE 5/6 before Update 15-22. JavaFX has been an independent artifact since Java 11 and
      is not OpenJDK; the match is wrong by construction, not merely stale.

      Suppressed by CPE rather than by CVE on purpose: the defect is the identifier, so pinning today's seven
      CVEs would let the next JRE CVE published against `*:openjdk` back in. `oracle:javafx` is left live, so a
      genuine future JavaFX vulnerability still fails the gate.

      No `until`: a CPE mismatch is not a risk that expires. Revisit only if dependency-check's evidence
      collection changes or JavaFX stops shipping `com.sun.*` packages.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.openjfx/javafx-.*@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cpe>cpe:/a:oracle:openjdk</cpe>
    <cpe>cpe:/a:sun:openjdk</cpe>
  </suppress>

  <!-- ==========================================================================================
       2. RISK ACCEPTANCES

       None. Nothing in the shipped dependency graph currently carries an accepted CVE.
       ========================================================================================== -->

</suppressions>
